{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-130135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-4$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5716e843d004aee90df80f9a99a117edc8c2365c713bab3e5b490fdeeedb9156"} {"question" "q-130135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Acade