esults in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and $x{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{-3x}+3x$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-90965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5716e843d004aee90df80f9a99a117edc8c2365c713bab3e5b490fdeeedb9156"} {"question" "q-90965" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-90965" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "2{e}^{-3x}+3x"] ["math" "3{e}^{-3x}-6x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "5x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}+x{e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-90965" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{-3x}+3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-5$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5x}$ and $x{e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5716e843d004aee90df80f9a99a117edc8c2365c713bab3e5b490fdeeedb9156"} {"question" "q-130146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10{e}^{-5x}+4x{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "8{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-5x}+x{e}^{-5x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -4)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and $x{e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5716e843d004aee90df80f9a99a117edc8c2365c713bab3e5b490fdeeedb9156"} {"question" "q-130150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "12{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "2{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "q-130150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73e7ae59f61c6f3185b23239fe580ad4bcebe310317fb318db369ebaebca9f8"} {"question" "e-6591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0.$ If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/example-6591.json" "file_sha256" "2a9973875a44e4a9f094f2aa4f8a0606173e7617521667105b0297ca120aef4e"} {"question" "e-6591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =  1$  is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/example-6591.json" "file_sha256" "2a9973875a44e4a9f094f2aa4f8a0606173e7617521667105b0297ca120aef4e"} {"question" "e-6592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/example-6592.json" "file_sha256" "914f87ef8caabcd2ea23344c329d60bab5263dfb0be262806d958a263ba10b02"} {"question" "e-6592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+36{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =  6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/example-6592.json" "file_sha256" "914f87ef8caabcd2ea23344c329d60bab5263dfb0be262806d958a263ba10b02"} {"question" "e-6593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$  for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{6x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/example-6593.json" "file_sha256" "8ccda63900106c6108d6e4b3ae55aae76c27116993524eeb9985e1214977cc89"} {"question" "e-6593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+36{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root.  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x},$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063871/example-6593.json" "file_sha256" "8ccda63900106c6108d6e4b3ae55aae76c27116993524eeb9985e1214977cc89"}] "usage" {"q-7795" [] "q-64798" [] "e-6591" [] "e-6593" [] "e-6592" []} "basis" 2070 "retractions" [[17592186088291 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{9}{4}y = 0$."] [17592186088181 :question/problem "Given the equation $y^{″} - 14y^{′} + 49y = 0$ for $y = y(t)$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?"] [17592186087531 :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0$. If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda$?"] [17592186087531 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} - 2λe^{λx} + e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda = 1$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."] [17592186087532 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0$."] [17592186087532 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} - 12λe^{λt} + 36e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} - 12λ + 36) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 12λ + 36 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 6)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}\n$$"] [17592186087533 :question/problem "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$ for $y=y(x)$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $xe^{-6x}$\n2. $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n3. $2e^{6x}$"] [17592186087533 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} + 12λe^{λx} + 36e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} + 12λ + 36) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 12λ + 36 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 6)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x}$, which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186086018 17592186086019 17592186086020 17592186086021 17592186086022 17592186086677 17592186086678 17592186086679 17592186086680 17592186086681] "immutable_fields" [[17592186086017 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086017 :answer-field/id] [17592186086017 :answer-field/key] [17592186086017 :answer-field/type] [17592186086676 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086676 :answer-field/id] [17592186086676 :answer-field/key] [17592186086676 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
